Optimal Solution - Schedule tasks using a max-heap by executing the most frequent tasks first in fixed cycles of size (n + 1) to minimize idle time.

class Solution {
    public int leastInterval(char[] tasks, int n) {
        // Frequency array for tasks A–Z
        int[] freq = new int[26];
        for (char ch : tasks) {
            freq[ch - 'A']++;
        }
        // Max-heap to always pick the task with highest remaining frequency
        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq =
                new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
        // Add all non-zero frequencies to the heap
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            if (freq[i] > 0) {
                pq.offer(freq[i]);
            }
        }
        int time = 0;
        // Process tasks in cycles of length (n + 1)
        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            int taskCount = 0;          // tasks executed in this cycle
            int cycle = n + 1;
            List<Integer> store = new ArrayList<>();
            // Execute up to (n + 1) most frequent tasks
            while (cycle-- > 0 && !pq.isEmpty()) {
                int currFreq = pq.poll();
                // If task still has remaining count, store it for reinsertion
                if (currFreq > 1) {
                    store.add(currFreq - 1);
                }
                taskCount++;
            }
            // Push remaining tasks back into the heap
            for (int remaining : store) {
                pq.offer(remaining);
            }
            // If heap is empty, no idle time needed
            // Otherwise, full cycle length is used
            time += pq.isEmpty() ? taskCount : (n + 1);
        }
        return time;
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(N).
Space Complexity :- O(1).
